---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import StackBar from '../components/StackBar.astro';
import CostCurve from '../components/CostCurve.astro';
// READ-ONLY IMPORTS. This build touches no engine or data file: it reads two existing,
// already-sourced datasets so the viz below never hand-types a percentage that already
// has a home elsewhere on the site. See the VIZ DERIVATION block for how each is used.
import { hotelTaxMap, colours } from '../data/hotel-tax-map.js';
import { PROPERTY_FEE_BAND, HOTEL_EXTRAS } from '../data/booking-tactics.js';

const CHECKED = 'September 2026';
const CHECKED_ISO = '2026-09-01';

// THE JUMP LIST, same rail as /how-to-pay-less-on-hotels: one object read by both the
// nav and the sections, so a dead anchor is impossible by construction. "What this page
// sits on top of" is deliberately left out, the same way that page leaves its own closing
// material out of JUMP: it is a closing, cross-link section, not a destination a reader
// jumps to with a question.
const SEC = {
  stack:  { id: 'fee-stack', label: 'What is actually in an Airbnb price' },
  short:  { id: 'cleaning-fee-math', label: 'The cleaning fee, and short stays' },
  versus: { id: 'hotel-or-rental', label: 'When a hotel wins, and when it does not' },
  other:  { id: 'other-platforms', label: 'Other platforms, and why they are different' },
  legal:  { id: 'is-it-allowed', label: 'Is it even allowed where you are going' },
  facts:  { id: 'notable-facts', label: 'A few facts worth knowing' },
  range:  { id: 'the-real-range', label: 'So what should you actually compare' },
};
const JUMP = Object.values(SEC);

// ============================================================================
// THE VIZ DERIVATION. Every number the chart draws is computed here, not typed into
// the markup below, for the same reason /how-to-pay-less-on-hotels computes its own
// tax segment at build time: a hand-typed percentage is a second copy of a figure
// that already has a home, and a second copy is a copy that goes stale.
// ============================================================================

// THE HOTEL-TAX SEGMENT, read from hotel-tax-map.js and nowhere else. Both rows below
// use this SAME figure for their own tax segment, because in most cities the same
// municipal tourist tax applies whether you book a hotel or a short-term rental
// (Portugal and France's rates are the actual overlap the research memo names). Drawn
// at the median of every "checked" country on the map, captioned with the full range,
// the identical technique the sibling page's own tax segment uses. The two rows render
// it differently on purpose: full-strength on the rental row, faint on the hotel row,
// because a hotel's quoted rate often already has this folded in by law and a rental's
// usually does not. Same figure, two honest treatments; that contrast is the point.
const colouredEntries = hotelTaxMap.filter((e) => colours(e));
const taxPcts = colouredEntries.map((e) => e.addedPct).sort((a, b) => a - b);
const taxMedian = taxPcts[Math.floor(taxPcts.length / 2)];
const taxRangeLabel = `${taxPcts[0]} to ${taxPcts[taxPcts.length - 1]}%`;

// A DECLARED REFERENCE NIGHTLY RATE, and why it is $120 rather than borrowed from the
// hotel-tax map's own 150-euro basket. That basket was built for a European tax
// conversion and has no reason to also be the right assumption for a US-sourced
// cleaning-fee statistic. $120 is not a fresh assumption invented for this chart: it
// is the SAME illustrative nightly rate the cleaning-fee-math section below draws from
// the research memo's own amortisation example (onefinebnb.com), so this page rests on
// one declared assumption, stated once, rather than two disagreeing ones.
const VIZ_REF_NIGHTLY_USD = 120;
const CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD = 75;
const CLEAN_AVG_USD = 188;
const PLATFORM_PCT = 15.5;

const round1 = (n) => Math.round(n * 10) / 10;
// The cleaning fee, resort fee and platform fee below are all expressed as a percent
// of that declared $120 reference room, the same "index to a reference, never a real
// price" move the hotel-tax map itself uses for a flat charge with no percentage of
// its own.
const cleaningPct = round1((CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD / VIZ_REF_NIGHTLY_USD) * 100);
// A shared conversion, so every dollar figure below (resort fee, parking, platform fee)
// becomes a percent of the same declared $120 reference room through one formula
// rather than one hand-repeated calculation per figure.
const pctOfRef = (usd) => round1((usd / VIZ_REF_NIGHTLY_USD) * 100);

// THE RESORT-FEE FIGURE READS LIVE FROM PROPERTY_FEE_BAND (booking-tactics.js), the SAME
// resort-fee figure the sibling hotels page already cites, so this page can never quote a
// second, different number for the same mechanic. FLAGGED IN THE HANDBACK: this build's
// brief stated PROPERTY_FEE_BAND had already been corrected to $25 to $55 a night; the
// repo handed with this brief still carries the older $30 to $90 figure (and that older
// figure is ALSO still live in /hotel-tax-map's own prose and in hotel-tax-map.js's Aruba
// entry, a wider correction than this brief's file list covers). Rather than patch one of
// three copies myself and leave the other two contradicting it, this page reads
// PROPERTY_FEE_BAND exactly as it stands and will pick up the real figure automatically
// once that correction lands across all three places at once.
const resortLowPct = pctOfRef(PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortLowUsd);
const resortHighPct = pctOfRef(PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortHighUsd);
const resortMidPct = round1((resortLowPct + resortHighPct) / 2);

// THE PARKING FIGURE, read live from HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking (booking-tactics.js), never
// typed here. Drawn HATCHED, never solid: $44 is the average AMONG HOTELS THAT CHARGE
// for parking, most charge nothing, so this width is an illustrative range, not a
// measured line item. See the honesty-vocabulary caption on the page for what hatching
// means here.
const parkingCentralPct = pctOfRef(HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.centralUsd);
const parkingLowPct = pctOfRef(HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.lowUsd);
const parkingHighPct = pctOfRef(HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.highUsd);

const BASE_FILL = '#0A5644';
const CLEAN_FILL = '#B45309';
const PLATFORM_FILL = '#0E7A5F';
const TAX_FILL = '#7FB8A5';
const TAX_FAINT_FILL = '#D7ECE3';
const RESORT_FILL = '#E3B98A';
const PARKING_FILL = '#8C6A2F';

// A hatch-fill helper. Reuses StackBar's own per-instance hatch pattern (defined once,
// inside that component, keyed to whatever `id` a chart is given) rather than adding a
// second hatch mechanism: passing `url(#{id}-hatch)` as an ordinary segment's own `fill`
// draws that accumulating segment with the identical texture StackBar already uses
// elsewhere on the site for "this is not a solid, certain number." No change to
// StackBar.astro was needed for this; the component already renders whatever fill a
// segment is given.
const hatchFill = (barId) => `url(#${barId}-hatch)`;

const CHART1_ID = 'airbnb-hotel-compare';

const rentalTotal = Math.round(100 + cleaningPct + PLATFORM_PCT + taxMedian);
const hotelTotal = Math.round(100 + resortMidPct + parkingCentralPct + taxMedian);

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    label: 'A vacation rental, one night',
    segments: [
      { label: 'nightly rate', value: 100, fill: BASE_FILL, figure: '100' },
      {
        label: 'cleaning fee, flat, once per stay', value: cleaningPct, fill: CLEAN_FILL,
        figure: `$${CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD} median`,
      },
      {
        label: 'platform service fee', value: PLATFORM_PCT, fill: PLATFORM_FILL,
        figure: `${PLATFORM_PCT}%`,
      },
      {
        label: 'tax, on the booking or in cash later', value: taxMedian, fill: TAX_FILL,
        figure: taxRangeLabel,
      },
    ],
    totalFigure: String(rentalTotal),
    note: `Modeled against a $${VIZ_REF_NIGHTLY_USD} room for a single night, the same illustrative rate the cleaning-fee math below uses. NerdWallet's median cleaning fee, $${CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD} a stay, is already this large a slice on one night; the average runs far higher, $${CLEAN_AVG_USD} (AirROI), because a few large, expensive properties pull it up hard. The platform fee shown is Airbnb's own incoming host-only rate; historically it ran 14 to 16% and showed to the guest as its own line, and it is increasingly folded into the nightly rate instead as the site notes below. The tax segment uses the same figure as the hotel row, read from our own hotel tax map.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'A hotel, one night, all in',
    segments: [
      { label: 'room rate', value: 100, fill: BASE_FILL, figure: '100' },
      {
        label: 'resort fee, only at some properties', value: resortMidPct, fill: RESORT_FILL,
        figure: `$${PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortLowUsd} to $${PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortHighUsd}, not universal`,
      },
      {
        label: 'parking, where it is charged', value: parkingCentralPct, fill: hatchFill(CHART1_ID),
        figure: `$${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.centralUsd} typical ($${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.lowUsd} to $${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.highUsd})`,
      },
      {
        label: 'tax, often already inside the sticker', value: taxMedian, fill: TAX_FAINT_FILL,
        figure: taxRangeLabel,
      },
    ],
    totalFigure: String(hotelTotal),
    note: `Most hotels charge no resort fee at all; it is a US pattern at a minority of properties, drawn in a lighter color for that reason. Parking is hatched rather than solid on purpose: it is a real cost that lands on top, but $${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.centralUsd} is an illustrative typical figure, not a measured one, read the caption below the chart for why. The tax segment is the identical figure the rental row uses above, drawn faint here because in much of Europe a hotel's quoted rate already has this tax folded in by law, so it often never appears as a line at all. Same tax, two different treatments: that contrast is the actual point of drawing it twice.`,
    commissionNote: `Parking, hatched: ${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.honestyCaveat} ${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.aboveNote}`,
  },
];

// ============================================================================
// CHART 2. The SAME stacked-bar component and the SAME $120-reference model as Chart 1
// above, recomputed for a 30-night stay, with a THIRD row: a plain hotel that charges
// neither resort fee nor parking, so this chart can never be read as "every hotel adds
// this much." Three rows, one honest question: does 30 nights actually erase the
// rental's flat-fee penalty? The rental's cleaning fee is a ONE-TIME charge, so its
// per-night share shrinks with nights; the platform fee and tax are PERCENTAGES of the
// nightly rate, so they do not shrink, the same distinction the shared stay-length
// engine below is built on for Viz 3. A hotel's resort fee, parking and tax are all
// per-night recurring charges too, so the hotel bars barely change between Chart 1 and
// Chart 2; only the rental bar genuinely moves.
// ============================================================================
const CHART2_NIGHTS = 30;
const CHART2_ID = 'airbnb-hotel-compare-30';
const cleaningPctAt30 = round1(((CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD / CHART2_NIGHTS) / VIZ_REF_NIGHTLY_USD) * 100);

const rental30Total = Math.round(100 + cleaningPctAt30 + PLATFORM_PCT + taxMedian);
const plainHotel30Total = Math.round(100 + taxMedian);
const feeHotel30Total = Math.round(100 + resortMidPct + parkingCentralPct + taxMedian);

// CHART 2's HONESTY, ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The whole point of this chart is that
// 30 nights erodes MOST of the rental's cleaning-fee penalty but not all of it: the
// rental must still NOT beat a plain hotel outright, and it must still land clearly
// below a hotel that charges resort and parking fees. Both are consequences of the
// figures above, not hand-tuned, and a future edit to any constant this chart reads
// could break either claim silently, so the build fails instead of shipping a chart
// that quietly overclaims.
if (rental30Total <= plainHotel30Total) {
  throw new Error('airbnb-real-cost: Chart 2 has the 30-night rental beating a plain hotel outright ('
    + rental30Total + ' <= ' + plainHotel30Total + '). The honesty spine requires the rental to land '
    + 'ABOVE a plain hotel, not below it; check CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD, PLATFORM_PCT, taxMedian or CHART2_NIGHTS.');
}
if (rental30Total >= feeHotel30Total) {
  throw new Error('airbnb-real-cost: Chart 2 does not have the 30-night rental clearly below a '
    + 'fee-charging hotel (' + rental30Total + ' >= ' + feeHotel30Total + '). Check PROPERTY_FEE_BAND, '
    + 'HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking or the other Chart 2 constants.');
}

const chart2Rows = [
  {
    label: 'A vacation rental, 30 nights',
    segments: [
      { label: 'nightly rate', value: 100, fill: BASE_FILL, figure: '100' },
      {
        label: 'cleaning fee, one time, spread thin', value: cleaningPctAt30, fill: CLEAN_FILL,
        figure: `$${CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD} once, over 30 nights`,
      },
      { label: 'platform service fee', value: PLATFORM_PCT, fill: PLATFORM_FILL, figure: `${PLATFORM_PCT}%` },
      { label: 'tax, on the booking or in cash later', value: taxMedian, fill: TAX_FILL, figure: taxRangeLabel },
    ],
    totalFigure: String(rental30Total),
    note: `The one-time cleaning fee that made up ${cleaningPct}% of a single night above now makes up only ${cleaningPctAt30}%, spread across 30 nights instead of one. The platform fee and tax do not shrink the same way: both are percentages of the nightly rate, so they charge the same share every night regardless of how long you stay.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'A hotel, 30 nights, no resort or parking fee',
    segments: [
      { label: 'room rate', value: 100, fill: BASE_FILL, figure: '100' },
      { label: 'tax, often already inside the sticker', value: taxMedian, fill: TAX_FAINT_FILL, figure: taxRangeLabel },
    ],
    totalFigure: String(plainHotel30Total),
    note: 'A hotel that charges neither a resort fee nor for parking. Its own charges are all per-night, so this total barely differs from a one-night stay: nothing here amortizes the way a one-time fee does.',
  },
  {
    label: 'A hotel, 30 nights, resort fee and parking',
    segments: [
      { label: 'room rate', value: 100, fill: BASE_FILL, figure: '100' },
      {
        label: 'resort fee, only at some properties', value: resortMidPct, fill: RESORT_FILL,
        figure: `$${PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortLowUsd} to $${PROPERTY_FEE_BAND.usResortHighUsd}, not universal`,
      },
      {
        label: 'parking, where it is charged', value: parkingCentralPct, fill: hatchFill(CHART2_ID),
        figure: `$${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.centralUsd} typical ($${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.lowUsd} to $${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.highUsd})`,
      },
      { label: 'tax, often already inside the sticker', value: taxMedian, fill: TAX_FAINT_FILL, figure: taxRangeLabel },
    ],
    totalFigure: String(feeHotel30Total),
    note: 'The same resort-fee and parking figures as Chart 1, unchanged by nights, because both are charged per night rather than once per stay.',
    commissionNote: `Parking, hatched: ${HOTEL_EXTRAS.parking.honestyCaveat}`,
  },
];

// ============================================================================
// THE SHARED STAY-LENGTH ENGINE, for Chart 3 (the crossover bands). One function, one
// set of declared inputs, called four different ways (two party sizes times two
// cleaning-fee edges).
// ============================================================================
//
// THE THREE COMPARISON FIGURES ARE PROMOTED HERE FROM PROSE TO DECLARED CONSTANTS.
// They already appeared as plain numbers in the "When a hotel wins" section below
// (NerdWallet's own comparison: hotel room $89, small Airbnb $125, large Airbnb $60 a
// person for six travelers). Declaring them here and having that paragraph read from
// the SAME constants, rather than typing the numbers twice, is the one-fact-one-home
// rule this whole brief turns on: three visuals telling one story need one data source.
const HOTEL_USD = 89;
const SOLO_BASE_USD = 125;
const GROUP_BASE_USD = 60;
const SOLO_PARTY = 2;
const GROUP_PARTY = 6;

// Every stay-length number is an INDEX against the hotel, held at 100, never a dollar
// figure on any axis. idx() is the one place a dollar becomes an index point.
const HOTEL_IDX = 100;
const idx = (usd) => Math.round((usd / HOTEL_USD) * HOTEL_IDX * 10) / 10;

// effectivePerNightIdx(baseUsd, partySize, cleaningFeeUsd, nights): the ONE shared
// helper Chart 3 calls, four ways. A flat cleaning fee, charged once per stay, is
// spread across every night AND across every traveler splitting that one listing, so
// its bite per person per night is cleaningFeeUsd / (partySize * nights). Add that to
// the base per-person nightly rate and convert to the same hotel-referenced index
// everything else on this page uses.
const effectivePerNightIdx = (baseUsd, partySize, cleaningFeeUsd, nights) =>
  idx(baseUsd + cleaningFeeUsd / (partySize * nights));

// AXIS FIX FROM THE PRIOR BUILD: this ran to 14 nights and Gary rejected it, agreed at
// 30 and never shipped. 30 it is.
const NIGHTS = Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => i + 1);
const NIGHT_TICKS = [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 10, 14, 20, 25, 30];

const hotelLineIdx = NIGHTS.map(() => HOTEL_IDX);
const HOTEL_LINE_COLOR = '#0A5644';
const SOLO_LINE_COLOR = '#B45309';
const GROUP_LINE_COLOR = '#0E7A5F';

// CHART 3, DRAWN AS BANDS. Each rental series is a spread from the median-cleaning-fee
// edge to the average-cleaning-fee edge of that SAME party size (the research memo's
// own explanation for why AirROI's average sits so far above NerdWallet's median is
// that large, pricier properties pull it up, and a large group books exactly that kind
// of property), which is the honest way to show "the range across different
// properties" without inventing a second, unsourced axis for how nightly rates vary by
// city. The hotel stays a single flat reference.
const soloBandIdx = NIGHTS.map((n) => ({
  low: effectivePerNightIdx(SOLO_BASE_USD, SOLO_PARTY, CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD, n),
  high: effectivePerNightIdx(SOLO_BASE_USD, SOLO_PARTY, CLEAN_AVG_USD, n),
}));
const groupBandIdx = NIGHTS.map((n) => ({
  low: effectivePerNightIdx(GROUP_BASE_USD, GROUP_PARTY, CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD, n),
  high: effectivePerNightIdx(GROUP_BASE_USD, GROUP_PARTY, CLEAN_AVG_USD, n),
}));

// WHERE THE GROUP BAND ACTUALLY STRADDLES THE HOTEL LINE, computed rather than eyeballed,
// so the prose naming that night can never drift from the chart drawing it.
const groupOverlapNights = NIGHTS.filter((n, i) => groupBandIdx[i].low < HOTEL_IDX && groupBandIdx[i].high > HOTEL_IDX);
const soloEverOverlaps = soloBandIdx.some((b) => b.low < HOTEL_IDX);

// CHART 3'S HONESTY, ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The couple/solo band must never dip
// below the hotel line (its lower, cheaper edge included), and the group band must
// start above the hotel and cross below within the first couple of nights, exactly the
// honesty spine this whole build is built on. Both are consequences of the constants
// above, not hand-tuned, so a future edit that quietly breaks either claim fails the
// build instead of shipping a chart that overclaims.
if (soloEverOverlaps) {
  throw new Error('airbnb-real-cost: Chart 3\'s solo/couple band dips below the hotel index. The honesty '
    + 'spine requires it to stay above the hotel line at every night modelled; check SOLO_BASE_USD, '
    + 'SOLO_PARTY, CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD, CLEAN_AVG_USD or HOTEL_USD.');
}
if (groupOverlapNights.length === 0 || groupOverlapNights[0] !== NIGHTS[0]) {
  throw new Error('airbnb-real-cost: Chart 3\'s group band does not start straddling the hotel index on the '
    + 'first night modelled and then drop below. Check GROUP_BASE_USD, GROUP_PARTY, CLEAN_MEDIAN_USD, '
    + 'CLEAN_AVG_USD or HOTEL_USD.');
}
if (groupBandIdx.some((b, i) => NIGHTS[i] > groupOverlapNights[groupOverlapNights.length - 1] && b.high >= HOTEL_IDX)) {
  throw new Error('airbnb-real-cost: Chart 3\'s group band pokes back above the hotel index after it '
    + 'first dropped below, which the honesty spine does not expect. Check the constants feeding groupBandIdx.');
}

const viz3Series = [
  { key: 'hotel', label: 'Hotel, flat reference', kind: 'reference', color: HOTEL_LINE_COLOR, values: hotelLineIdx },
  {
    key: 'solo', label: 'Rental, solo or a couple', kind: 'band', color: SOLO_LINE_COLOR, values: soloBandIdx,
    note: 'the whole band stays above the hotel line, on these figures',
  },
  {
    key: 'group', label: 'Rental, a group of six', kind: 'band', color: GROUP_LINE_COLOR, values: groupBandIdx,
    note: groupOverlapNights.length
      ? `overlaps the hotel line at night ${groupOverlapNights.join(', ')}, then drops fully below it`
      : 'drops fully below the hotel line',
  },
];

const webPageLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'The real cost of an Airbnb',
  description: 'The fee stack behind a short-term rental total, the cleaning-fee math on short stays, when a hotel wins instead, and the one thing worth checking on any listing before you book.',
  url: 'https://truetripcosts.com/airbnb-real-cost',
  // dateModified is OMITTED, deliberately. It was bound to this page's re-check date, so
  // every routine re-check bumped it, which is artificial freshening: penalized, no
  // ranking benefit, and untrue. This page aggregates facts from many countries and has
  // no curated change record of its own, so there is no honest Changed date to bind to.
  // Absence is not zero. datePublished is untouched.
};
---
<Base
  title="The real cost of an Airbnb: the fee stack, the cleaning fee math, and when a hotel wins | True Trip Costs"
  description="What actually lands on a short-term rental bill: the nightly rate, a flat cleaning fee (NerdWallet's median is $75, not the $150 to $270 horror stories), a platform service fee moving from 14 to 16 percent to a host-only 15.5 percent, and tax that is sometimes collected in cash on arrival. When a hotel beats it and when it does not, how VRBO differs, and the one thing worth checking on every listing before you book. Not a legality database, not a booking site.">
  <Nav />
  <main class="wrap">

    <Hero
      eyebrow="Where you stay"
      title="The real cost of an Airbnb."
      stamp={`Checked ${CHECKED}`}
      slim={true} />

    <section class="bt-prose">
      <p class="bt-lede">The nightly rate you see is almost never the number you pay. A flat cleaning fee lands once, however long you stay. A platform fee sits inside or beside the price, and it is moving from beside to inside right now. Tax sometimes waits for you in cash at the door. Airbnb is the front door of this page, because it is where most US travelers start looking, but the same fee stack follows you to VRBO and to smaller platforms too, and on those it is usually less visible, not more.</p>

      <div class="bt-quick">
        <p class="bt-qk">The 30-second version</p>
        <ol class="bt-qlist">
          <li><a href="#fee-stack">See the full price broken into its four parts</a>, nightly rate, cleaning fee, platform fee and tax, before you compare it against anything else.</li>
          <li><a href="#cleaning-fee-math">A flat cleaning fee hits a one or two night stay hardest</a>, and fades fast the longer you stay.</li>
          <li>Solo or on a short trip, <a href="#hotel-or-rental">open the hotel total too</a>. It wins more often than people expect.</li>
          <li><a href="#is-it-allowed">Rules vary by city and change constantly.</a> Look for a license or registration number on the listing before you book anything.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="bt-qfoot">Airbnb's own fee model is mid-migration through September and October 2026 as you read this, so if it is later than that, recheck <a href="#fee-stack">the fee-stack section</a> first: the mechanics below are dated for exactly that reason.</p>
      </div>

      <details class="bt-not">
        <summary class="bt-not-sum">
          <span class="bt-not-k">What this page is not</span>
          <span class="bt-not-one">It is not a legality database, and it never will be.</span>
          <svg class="bt-not-chev" width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </summary>
        <p>We do not track city by city rental caps, bans or registration rules, because they change on a timescale of months and a stale legality claim could cost you more than the fee stack ever would. We do not compare live prices between platforms, and we will never tell you which one to book on. Where we explain how a platform's fee works, that is mechanism, not a recommendation, the same rule our hotel-tactics page holds itself to.</p>
      </details>

      <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="lab">On this page</p>
        <ul>
          {JUMP.map((j) => (
            <li><a href={`#${j.id}`}>{j.label}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.stack.id}>
      <h2>What is actually in an Airbnb price</h2>
      <p class="bt-note">Four things build the total, and only the first one is what you searched for. Airbnb has shown the all-in total by default since April 2025, before tax. That is real progress, and it is also why this page still exists: the total is more visible now, not simpler.</p>

      <div class="arc-stack">
        <div class="arc-stack-item">
          <p class="arc-stack-k">1. The nightly rate</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">Shown all-in, including fees but before tax, to every user by default since April 21, 2025, when Airbnb retired the old opt-in toggle. What you see in search is closer to the real number than it used to be, but taxes can still be added at the final checkout step, or on arrival. See <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/21/airbnb-will-automatically-display-total-price-to-all-users/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airbnb's April 2025 total-price rollout</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="arc-stack-item">
          <p class="arc-stack-k">2. The cleaning fee</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">Flat, charged once no matter how many nights you book. <a href="https://www.nerdwallet.com/travel/learn/airbnb-cleaning-fees-brutal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NerdWallet's 2022 study</a> of 1,000 US entire-place bookings put the median at $75 a stay, with 14% of listings charging none at all. <a href="https://www.airroi.com/blog/airbnb-cleaning-fee-economics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AirROI's larger sample</a> of active listings found a much higher average, $188, pulled up hard by a smaller number of large, expensive properties. A median and an average that far apart is the tell: this page gives you a range, on purpose, never one number.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="arc-stack-item">
          <p class="arc-stack-k">3. The platform service fee</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">Historically 14% to 16% of the subtotal under Airbnb's old split-fee model, shown to the guest as its own line. Airbnb is retiring that split. Under the new host-only model the host alone pays 15.5% (16% in Mexico and Brazil), and the guest increasingly never sees a separate fee line at all, it gets folded into the nightly rate instead. The cutover reaches hosts outside the European Economic Area on September 15, 2026, and hosts in the EEA and Switzerland on October 13, 2026, per Airbnb's own July 2026 announcement, <a href="https://www.rentalscaleup.com/airbnb-host-fees/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reported at Rental Scale-Up</a>. Worth rechecking after those dates. VRBO runs a different shape entirely: see <a href="#other-platforms">other platforms</a> below.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="arc-stack-item">
          <p class="arc-stack-k">4. Tax</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">Sometimes Airbnb or VRBO collects it automatically at booking, wherever the platform holds a collection agreement with the local authority. Where it does not, the host or the municipality collects it separately, often on arrival, often in cash. Airbnb <a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/1036" target="_blank" rel="noopener">says plainly it collects in specific jurisdictions</a> and that a host <a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/2496" target="_blank" rel="noopener">may still need to collect other taxes manually</a>, which is the actual mechanism behind the pay-in-cash-at-check-in surprise. The booking total you saw is not always the last number.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bt-figure">
        <p class="arc-figh">A vacation rental against a hotel, one night, all in on both sides</p>
        <p class="bt-note">Both rows are indexed to the same assumed nightly rate, so this is a shape, not a price. Whatever your actual room costs, that is the 100. No dollar total is implied for either one.</p>

        <p class="arc-vocab"><b>Solid</b> is a width we sourced. <b>Hatched</b> is an illustrative width from a sourced typical range, check your own rate, never a per-trip number. A <b>flag</b> below the chart, with no width at all, is a real line we deliberately do not size.</p>

        <StackBar
          id={CHART1_ID}
          rows={comparisonRows}
          axisMax={220}
          ticks={[0, 50, 100, 150, 200]}
          unitWord="percent of the same assumed nightly rate" />
        <p class="bt-finding">The flat cleaning fee is what flips a short stay toward the hotel, even before the hotel's own extras are counted. Once parking and a resort fee are added honestly, the hotel side is not free of fine print either; it is just a different kind of fine print than the rental's.</p>

        <div class="arc-flags">
          <p class="arc-flags-k">Flagged, not sized</p>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="arc-flag-tag">flag</span> <b>Hotel wifi:</b> {HOTEL_EXTRAS.wifi.note} {HOTEL_EXTRAS.wifi.doubleCountWarning}</li>
            <li><span class="arc-flag-tag value">value</span> <b>Hotel breakfast, if included:</b> {HOTEL_EXTRAS.breakfast.note}</li>
            <li><span class="arc-flag-tag included">included</span> <b>Rental parking and wifi:</b> usually included in the nightly rate, so there is close to nothing to add on this side.</li>
            <li><span class="arc-flag-tag not-provided">not provided</span> <b>Rental breakfast:</b> not part of the booking. No value to add, and nothing bundled to flag.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="arc-vizfoot">Ranges from NerdWallet, ResortFeeChecker/Elliott, and GSA, checked {CHECKED}. No live prices.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.short.id}>
      <h2>The cleaning fee, and why short stays feel it hardest</h2>
      <p class="bt-note">A cleaning fee is flat. It does not care whether you stay one night or ten, so its bite per night falls the longer the stay runs. This is an illustration of the shape, not a quote for your own trip: real nightly rates and real cleaning fees both vary too much to promise one number.</p>
      <p class="bt-finding">Illustratively: a $120-a-night listing with a $150 cleaning fee works out to an effective $270 a night for a single night, but only $141 a night spread across a week. Same listing, same fee, a very different number depending only on how long you stay.</p>
      <p class="bt-note">That is the whole mechanic behind <a href="#hotel-or-rental">why a hotel often wins on a short, solo trip</a>: not because a rental is a bad deal, but because a fee designed to be paid once lands entirely on whichever nights you actually book. Source: <a href="https://onefinebnb.com/airbnb-cleaning-fee-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">One Fine BNB's cleaning-fee amortisation example</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.versus.id}>
      <h2>When a hotel wins, and when it does not</h2>
      <p class="bt-note">The honest answer is platform-neutral: it depends entirely on the shape of the trip, not on which platform you searched first.</p>
      <p class="arc-stack-v">For a short stay, one or two nights, solo or as a couple, a hotel very often wins once the cleaning fee and service fee are added in. <a href="https://www.nerdwallet.com/travel/learn/airbnb-for-large-groups" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NerdWallet's own comparison</a> put a single hotel room about 29% cheaper than a small Airbnb, per person, for two travelers (median nightly figures in that study: hotel room ${HOTEL_USD}, small Airbnb ${SOLO_BASE_USD}). Flip the group size and the finding flips too: for six travelers, a large rental ran about 33% cheaper per person than three separate hotel rooms (large Airbnb ${GROUP_BASE_USD} a person, hotel rooms averaging ${HOTEL_USD} apiece). Neither number is a rule for your trip specifically, both are what one real study found. Solo and short leans hotel. Bigger and longer leans rental. Open the all-in total both ways and compare, every time, rather than assuming either one wins on principle.</p>

      <div class="bt-figure">
        <p class="arc-figh">The same bars, 30 nights instead of one</p>
        <p class="bt-note">Same component, same colors, same method as the chart above, just a longer stay, and a hotel now shown two honest ways: one that adds nothing beyond the room, one that charges what the fee-stack above actually catalogs. Solid, hatched and flag mean the same thing here as above.</p>
        <StackBar
          id={CHART2_ID}
          rows={chart2Rows}
          axisMax={220}
          ticks={[0, 50, 100, 150, 200]}
          unitWord="percent of the same assumed nightly rate" />
        <p class="bt-finding">Thirty nights erases most of the rental's flat cleaning fee. It lands just above a plain hotel with no extras, not below it, and well below a hotel that charges for parking and a resort fee. Length helps the rental a great deal. It does not make the rental free, and it does not make it automatically win.</p>
        <p class="bt-note">This is one representative property on each side. Real listings and real hotels vary property to property and city to city, which is exactly what the next chart shows.</p>
        <p class="arc-vizfoot">Ranges from NerdWallet, ResortFeeChecker/Elliott, and GSA, checked {CHECKED}. Not your quote, no live prices.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.other.id}>
      <h2>Other platforms, and why they are different</h2>
      <p class="arc-stack-v">Airbnb is the front door of this page because it is the platform most US travelers search first, and because its own fee mechanics are the best documented. The same fee stack, cleaning fee, platform fee, tax, applies just as much on VRBO and on smaller or direct-owner platforms. What actually differs is visibility. Airbnb now shows the all-in total by default. <a href="https://www.rentalrecon.com/fees/vrbo-service-fee-for-guests/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VRBO's own guest service fee</a> is a sliding scale, commonly 6% to 15% of the subtotal, and it falls as the size of the booking grows, the opposite shape from a flat cleaning fee. Smaller platforms and direct-owner bookings often show the least of it up front of all. The watching this page is about pays off more, not less, the further you get from Airbnb's own search results.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.legal.id}>
      <h2>Is it even allowed where you are going?</h2>
      <div class="recentchange arc-legal">
        <span class="rcpill">Evergreen warning, not a database</span>
        <p>Short-term rental rules, caps, registration requirements and outright bans move on a timescale of months, not years. Amsterdam cut its own citywide cap again in April 2026. The EU's own registration requirement only started binding on May 20, 2026. A city's rule can change between when we write this sentence and when you read it, so we do not keep a list, and we would rather tell you that plainly than hand you a number that used to be true and let you rely on it.</p>
        <p>Two places actually stay current, so check there instead of here:</p>
        <ul class="arc-legal-links">
          <li><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/resources/hosting-homes/a/learn-about-hosting-regulations-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airbnb's own per-country Responsible Hosting pages</a>, maintained by Airbnb itself and pointing at local rules city by city.</li>
          <li>The city or country's own official short-term-rental registration portal, usually one search away by name.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>One thing is worth checking on every listing everywhere, and it does not depend on knowing any one city's specific rule: does it show a license or registration number? Under a wave of new requirements, the EU from May 2026, Italy's CIN, Greece's AMA, Japan's minpaku number and more, platforms are now required to verify that number and can delist a listing that lacks one. A listing with no visible number can be pulled with little notice, sometimes close to your trip. It is arguably the single most useful, most durable safety signal on this whole page, precisely because it travels to any city rather than expiring with one city's rule.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.facts.id}>
      <h2>A few facts worth knowing</h2>
      <p class="bt-note">Three facts, not a database. Each is dated and stable enough to state plainly; anything less stable than this lives only in the warning above.</p>

      <div class="arc-facts">
        <div class="arc-fact">
          <p class="arc-fact-k">Mexico: Cancun, Playa del Carmen, Tulum (Quintana Roo)</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-v">Airbnb collects Quintana Roo's state lodging tax and Mexico's federal VAT automatically at booking. A separate municipal Environmental Sanitation fee does not travel through Airbnb at all: it is collected at the property, often in cash, at check-in or check-out. In 2026 it runs roughly 36 pesos per room per night in most municipalities, and more in Cancun specifically, where our own sources disagree, 76 versus 83 pesos, most likely a reflection of the fee's annual February reindexing. Bring small cash for it.</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-checked">Checked {CHECKED}. The peso amount re-indexes every February, so recheck around then.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="arc-fact">
          <p class="arc-fact-k">Italy, Greece and Japan, and the EU from May 2026</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-v">Since 2025, every short-term rental listing in Italy needs a National Identification Code (CIN) displayed, and platforms must delist listings that lack one. Greece runs the same idea under its own AMA registration number, Japan under its minpaku registration number, and the EU's Regulation 2024/1028 extends a version of the same requirement across all 27 member states from May 20, 2026. None of this proves a listing is legal everywhere else; a visible number is a floor, not a guarantee. But no visible number, anywhere, is a real warning sign.</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-checked">Checked {CHECKED}. The requirement itself is durable; re-verify specific city rules separately.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="arc-fact">
          <p class="arc-fact-k">European cities: the same tourist tax as the hotel next door</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-v">In most European cities that charge hotels a nightly tourist tax, a short-term rental usually owes the same tax, whether the platform collects it automatically or the host has to. We already track those rates for hotels, city by city, so we point at that dataset here rather than keeping a second copy that can drift out of sync with the first.</p>
          <p class="arc-fact-checked">See <a href="/hotel-taxes-and-fees">hotel taxes and fees</a> for the current rates.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec" id={SEC.range.id}>
      <h2>So what should you actually compare</h2>
      <p class="bt-note">The two bar charts above each compare one representative property against one representative hotel. Real listings do not all sit on that one line: cleaning fees, nightly rates and party sizes all vary, property to property and city to city. Drawn as a range instead of a single line, here is where the general rule genuinely bends.</p>

      <div class="bt-figure">
        <p class="arc-figh">The same comparison, drawn as a range instead of a line</p>
        <p class="bt-note">Each rental band runs from a typical, median-fee property (its lower, cheaper edge) to a larger, pricier property at the higher average fee (its upper edge), the same two figures the fee stack above cites. Where a band crosses the dashed hotel line, the general rule breaks for that length of stay.</p>
        <CostCurve
          id="airbnb-range-curve"
          nights={NIGHTS}
          xTicks={NIGHT_TICKS}
          series={viz3Series}
          axisMax={250}
          ticks={[0, 50, 100, 150, 200, 250]}
          unitWord="index points, the hotel held at 100" />

        <div class="arc-rule-bends">
          <p class="arc-rule-bends-k">Where the rule bends</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">A group of six: on the very first night, the band actually straddles the hotel line. A large, pricier property with a heavy cleaning fee can still lose to three hotel rooms on night one. By night {groupOverlapNights.length ? groupOverlapNights[groupOverlapNights.length - 1] + 1 : 2}, the whole band has dropped below the hotel and stays there. In pricier hotel markets, where a hotel's own tax or resort fee commonly adds more on top of its quoted rate (see the fee stack and the tax range above), that hotel-side addition tilts the picture further toward the rental still. In markets where hotel rooms run cheap and a flat cleaning fee is a proportionally bigger bite, the opposite: the crossover can take longer than one night.</p>
          <p class="arc-stack-v">A solo traveler or a couple: on these figures, the band never dips below the hotel line, at any length of stay we modeled, out to a full month. If you are two people hoping a month-long rental will beat the hotel outright, the honest answer is that it usually will not, though it gets closer to break-even the longer you stay. We would rather tell you that plainly than let the earlier chart's downward slope imply otherwise.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="arc-vizfoot">Ranges from NerdWallet and industry fee studies, checked {CHECKED}. Not your quote, no live prices.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="bt-note">None of this is a calculator. It is the shape of the two things worth comparing before you book: how many nights, and how many people. Open the real, all-in total for both, every time, the same rule the chart above the fee stack already makes.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="bt-sec arc-closer">
      <h2>What this page sits on top of</h2>
      <p class="arc-stack-v">The government tax mechanics here are the same ones we track for hotels at <a href="/hotel-taxes-and-fees">hotel taxes and fees</a>. The booking-moment tactics, comparing properly, checking a property's own site, reading past drip pricing, live at <a href="/how-to-pay-less-on-hotels">how to pay less on hotels</a>. And the full trip math, airfare, lodging, everyday spending, all in one place, is at <a href="/calculator">our calculator</a>.</p>
      <p class="arc-stack-v">We are not selling you a room, on Airbnb, VRBO or anywhere else. <span class="tg free">we earn nothing</span> from where you choose to stay.</p>
    </section>

